As a [[Premier League]] club, [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] started in the third round where they were drawn against [[EFL Championship|Championship]] team [[Rotherham United F.C.|Rotherham United]] at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]].  In what Neil Johnston of the [[BBC]] described as a "powerful attacking performance", City dominated their opponents and won 7–0, with goals from [[Raheem Sterling]], [[Phil Foden]], an [[own goal]] from [[Semi Ajayi]], [[Gabriel Jesus]], [[Riyad Mahrez]], [[Nicolás Otamendi]], and [[Leroy Sané]].<ref name=rufc>{{cite web |last=Johnston |first=Neil |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46713168 |title=Man City 7–0 Rotherham United |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=6 January 2019 |access-date=15 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508054643/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46713168 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  It was Rotherham's heaviest ever FA Cup defeat, but their manager [[Paul Warne]] was philosophical: "It was a difficult day but we were playing against a world-class team.  I don't think we embarrassed ourselves".<ref name=rufc/> In the fourth round, City were drawn at home once again, this time against fellow Premier League team [[Burnley F.C.|Burnley]].  In a display which Burnley manager [[Sean Dyche]] called "clinical", City won 5–0 with goals from Jesus, [[Bernardo Silva]], [[Kevin De Bruyne]], [[Sergio Agüero]] and an own goal from [[Kevin Long (footballer)|Kevin Long]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Emons |first=Michael |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46928656 |title=Manchester City 5–0 Burnley |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 January 2019 |access-date=15 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190820165651/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46928656 |archive-date=20 August 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the fifth round, City played [[EFL League Two|League Two]] side [[Newport County A.F.C.|Newport County]] away at [[Rodney Parade]] in [[Newport, Wales]].  Once again, City dominated their opponents, and although the first half ended goalless, they won 4–1 courtesy of two goals from Foden, and one each from Sané and Mahrez, with [[Pádraig Amond]] scoring the consolation for Newport.<ref>{{cite web |last=Pearlman |first=Michael |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47180807 |title=Newport 1–4 Man City |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 February 2019 |access-date=6 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190414013149/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47180807 |archive-date=14 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the quarter-finals, City drew Championship team [[Swansea City A.F.C.|Swansea City]].  Played at the [[Liberty Stadium]], Swansea were 2–0 ahead at half time, but a goal from Silva and an own goal from [[Kristoffer Nordfeldt]] were followed by a controversial late winner from Agüero.  Video replays demonstrated that the striker was [[offside (association football)|offside]], but as the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR) system was not in use at the Liberty Stadium, the goal was allowed to stand, and the Manchester club progressed with a 3–2 victory.<ref>{{cite web |last=Skelton |first=Jack |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47505051 |title=Swansea City 2–3 Manchester City |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 March 2019 |access-date=6 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190406153949/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47505051 |archive-date=6 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the semi-final, played at [[Wembley Stadium]] as a [[Glossary of association football terms#N|neutral venue]], Manchester City faced Premier League side [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]].  City progressed to the final after a 1–0 win with a Jesus goal from a De Bruyne cross in the fourth minute.<ref name=mancbha>{{cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil |author-link=Phil McNulty |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47756588 |title=Manchester City 1–0 Brighton & Hove Albion |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=6 April 2019 |access-date=6 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190406185951/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47756588 |archive-date=6 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>
