As a Premier League club, [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] also started in the third round where they faced [[National League South]] side [[Woking F.C.|Woking]] away at the [[Kingfield Stadium]].  There were 110 places in the [[English football league system]] between the clubs, and Watford dominated the match.  They won 2–0 with goals from [[Will Hughes]] and [[Troy Deeney]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Sanders |first=Emma |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46713166 |title=Woking 0–2 Watford |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=6 January 2019 |access-date=16 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191024210403/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46713166 |archive-date=24 October 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the next round Watford played fellow Premier League side [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] away at [[St James' Park]].  A total of eighteen changes were made to the starting line-ups of the two teams.  After a goalless first half, [[Andre Gray]] opened the scoring for Watford, and [[Isaac Success]]' strike in [[Association football#Duration and tie-breaking methods|injury time]] ensured Watford's progress with a 2–0 win.<ref>{{cite web |last=Mallows |first=Thomas |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46928655 |title=Newcastle United 0–2 Watford |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 January 2019 |access-date=16 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190421103014/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46928655 |archive-date=21 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the fifth round Watford played Championship side [[Queens Park Rangers F.C.|Queens Park Rangers]] away at [[Loftus Road]].  Watford won 1–0 when [[Étienne Capoue]] scored with his side's only shot on target of the game, just before half time.<ref>{{cite web |last=Johnston |first=Neil |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47166869 |title=Queens Park Rangers 0–1 Watford |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 February 2019 |access-date=16 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190420191641/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47166869 |archive-date=20 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  In the quarter-final, they played Premier League [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] at home at [[Vicarage Road]].  Watford took the lead mid-way through the first half with a Capoue strike, but [[Michy Batshuayi]] levelled the score in the 62nd minute.  Gray, a second-half [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], then scored the winning goal within two minutes of his introduction, ensuring a 2–1 Watford victory and progression to the semi-final at Wembley.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bullin |first=Matt |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47505050 |title=Watford 2–1 Crystal Palace |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 March 2019 |access-date=16 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190411221253/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47505050 |archive-date=11 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> There they faced Premier League [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] (Wolves).  A [[Header (association football)|header]] from [[Matt Doherty (footballer, born 1992)|Matt Doherty]] and a [[Volley (association football)|volley]] from [[Raúl Jiménez]] put Wolves into a 2–0 lead before [[Gerard Deulofeu]] reduced the deficit with what BBC reporter [[Phil McNulty]] described as "an audacious angled flick" with eleven minutes of the game remaining.  Four minutes into injury time, Deeney levelled the score from the [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty spot]], forcing the game into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].  Deulofeu then scored his second in the 104th minute to ensure Watford's progression to the final with a 3–2 victory.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47763394 |title=Watford 3–2 Wolverhampton Wanderers |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |first=Phil |last=McNulty |author-link=Phil McNulty |date=7 April 2019 |access-date=15 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519175041/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47763394 |archive-date=19 May 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Background===
[[File:Kevin Friend.jpg|right|alt=Kevin Friend|upright=0.7|thumb|[[Kevin Friend]] ''(pictured in 2012)'' was the match referee.]]
This was Manchester City's eleventh [[List of FA Cup Finals|FA Cup Final]] and their first since losing 1–0 to [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] in the [[2013 FA Cup final|2013 final]].  City had won the FA Cup on five previous occasions, the most recent being the [[2011 FA Cup final|2011 final]] when they beat [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]] 1–0.<ref name=thefa>{{Cite web | url = http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | title = FA Cup Finals, 1872 – today | access-date = 9 July 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170613182709/http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals | archive-date = 13 June 2017 | url-status = dead }}</ref>  Watford qualified for their first FA Cup Final since [[1984 FA Cup final|1984]] when they lost 2–0 to [[Everton F.C.|Everton]].<ref name=thefa/><ref name=t>{{cite news |first1=Jason |last1=Burt |first2=Charlie |last2=Eccleshare |title=Watford produce one of the great FA Cup comebacks to deny Wolves and storm into first final since 1984 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/07/watford-vs-wolves-fa-cup-semi-final-live-score-latest-updates/ |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=7 April 2019 |access-date=7 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190407162927/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/07/watford-vs-wolves-fa-cup-semi-final-live-score-latest-updates/ |archive-date=7 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  In the two meetings between the clubs during the [[2018–19 Premier League]], City won 2–1 at Vicarage Road in December 2018 and 3–1 at the City of Manchester Stadium.  Watford had beaten Manchester City once in the previous seventeen meetings, including a losing streak of ten games going back to 2013.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/watford/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Manchester%20City/ | website = 11v11.com | access-date = 16 July 2020 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | title = Watford football club: record v Manchester City | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170807052147/https://www.11v11.com/teams/watford/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Manchester%20City/ | archive-date = 7 August 2017 | url-status = live }}</ref>  The league season ended with City as champions and Watford eleventh, forty-eight points behind.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/premier-league/2019/ | website = 11v11.com | access-date = 16 July 2020 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | title = Premier League end of season table for 2018–19 season | archive-date = 9 March 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210309231417/https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/premier-league/2019/ | url-status = live }}</ref>  It was City's second cup final of the season: in February they had won the [[2018–19 EFL Cup|2018–19 League Cup]] against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in a [[penalty shootout]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47268861 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | title = Chelsea 0–0 Manchester City | access-date = 15 July 2020 | date = 24 February 2019 | first = Phil | last = McNulty | authorlink = Phil McNulty| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190402075855/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47268861 | archive-date = 2 April 2019 | url-status = live }}</ref>  This meant that City were aiming to become the first club in English football history to win the [[Treble (association football)#Domestic trebles|domestic treble]] which came into existence with the creation of the [[EFL Cup|League Cup]] in the [[1960–61 in English football|1960–61 season]].<ref>{{Cite web| url = https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/17/manchester-city-winning-treble-would-fitting-reward-pep-guardiolas/| work = [[The Daily Telegraph]]| access-date = 16 July 2020| title = Manchester City winning the treble would be fitting reward for Pep Guardiola's limitless ambition| first = Paul| last = Hayward| author-link = Paul Hayward (journalist)| date = 18 May 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190520153432/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/17/manchester-city-winning-treble-would-fitting-reward-pep-guardiolas/| archive-date = 20 May 2019| url-status = live}}</ref>

The referee for the match was [[Kevin Friend]] representing the [[Leicestershire and Rutland County Football Association]].  He was promoted to the [[Select Group]] in 2009 and had previously officiated over Wembley matches including the [[2012 FA Community Shield]] and the [[2013 Football League Cup Final]].  Friend's [[assistant referee (association football)|assistants]] were Constantine Hatzidakis ([[Kent County Football Association]]) and Matthew Wilkes ([[Birmingham County Football Association]]).  The fourth official was [[Graham Scott (referee)|Graham Scott]] ([[Berks & Bucks Football Association]]), and the reserve assistant referee was Edward Smart (Kent County Football Association).  [[Andre Marriner]] (Birmingham County Football Association) was the video assistant referee and was assisted by Harry Lennard ([[Sussex County Football Association]]).<ref name=referee>{{Cite web | url = http://www.thefa.com/news/2019/apr/29/fa-cup-final-referee-kevin-friend-290419 | title = Kevin Friend will referee 2019 Emirates FA Cup Final | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | access-date = 16 July 2020 | date = 29 April 2019 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190430032250/http://www.thefa.com/news/2019/apr/29/fa-cup-final-referee-kevin-friend-290419 | archive-date = 30 April 2019 | url-status = live}}</ref>  Both clubs received an allocation of approximately 28,000 tickets.  For adults, these were priced [[pound sterling|£]]45, £70, £115 and £145, with concessions in place.  14,000 tickets were distributed through the "football family", which included volunteers representing County FAs, FA-affiliated leagues, clubs and charities.  Manchester City supporters were seated on the east side of the ground, and Watford's on the west.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kick-off time and ticket details confirmed for 2019 Emirates FA Cup Final at Wembley |url=http://www.thefa.com/news/2019/apr/09/emirates-fa-cup-final-details-man-city-v-watford-090419 |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=9 April 2019 |access-date=12 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190410232547/http://www.thefa.com/news/2019/apr/09/emirates-fa-cup-final-details-man-city-v-watford-090419 |archive-date=10 April 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  The match was broadcast live in the UK on [[BBC One]] and [[BT Sport]].<ref name=referee/>  The traditional performance of the hymn "[[Abide with Me]]" was by the [[Band of the Scots Guards]] and a mixed choir.  Former players [[Luther Blissett]] (Watford) and [[Tony Book]] (Manchester City) brought out the [[FA Cup#Trophy|trophy]] before the teams were introduced to [[William, Prince of Wales|Prince William, Duke of Cambridge]].<ref name=guardlive3/>

Watford left-back [[José Holebas]] was [[sent off]] on the last day of the Premier League season in a 4–1 home defeat by [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]. The resulting one-match suspension would have ruled him out of the final but, on 13&nbsp;May, the red card was rescinded, clearing him for selection.<ref>{{cite news |title=Jose Holebas cleared to play in FA Cup final after red card overturned |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48259948 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 May 2019 |access-date=14 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190513204923/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48259948 |archive-date=13 May 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  Deulofeu had recovered from a [[dead leg]] sustained against West Ham.<ref name=guardlive/> The final was also slated to have been the Watford goalkeeper [[Heurelho Gomes]]' last professional football match, after he announced his intention to retire at the end of the season.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/15/watford-manager-javi-gracia-will-attempt-talk-goalkeeperheurelho/ |title=Watford manager Javi Gracia will attempt to talk goalkeeper Heurelho Gomes out of retirement plans |first = Jason | last = Burt| work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=15 May 2019 |access-date=17 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190517122432/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/15/watford-manager-javi-gracia-will-attempt-talk-goalkeeperheurelho/ |archive-date=17 May 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>  He was selected in preference to [[Ben Foster (footballer)|Ben Foster]], and [[Adrian Mariappa]] replaced [[Christian Kabasele]] in Watford's defence.<ref name=guardlive/>  Manchester City were still without long-term injured [[Benjamin Mendy]] but both [[Fernandinho (footballer, born May 1985)|Fernandinho]] and De Bruyne were available for selection,<ref name=guardlive/> the former having recovered from a knee injury while the latter was back from damaging his hamstring.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/manchestercity/fernandinho-could-return-for-man-city-in-fa-cup-final-with-watford-a4144691.html | work = [[Evening Standard]] | access-date = 29 July 2020 | title = Fernandinho could return for Man City in FA Cup Final with Watford | first = James | last = Robson | date = 17 May 2019 | archive-date = 8 June 2019 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190608163130/https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/manchestercity/fernandinho-could-return-for-man-city-in-fa-cup-final-with-watford-a4144691.html | url-status = live }}</ref>  Jesus was preferred up front with Agüero starting on the bench, along with De Bruyne.<ref name=guardlive/>  Watford played in their standard home [[Kit (association football)|kit]] of black and yellow stripes, black shorts and black socks while City's players wore light blue shirts, white shorts and white socks.<ref name=guardlive/>

===First half===
[[File:2019 FA Cup final.jpg|left|thumb|Pre-match display]]
The match was [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] by Watford just after 5&nbsp;p.m. on 18&nbsp;May 2019 in front of a Wembley crowd of 85,854.  The first chance of the game fell to [[Aymeric Laporte]] on 4 minutes whose long-range shot flew over Watford's crossbar.  Three minutes later, Bernardo Silva made a run but his pass into the Watford penalty area was intercepted by [[Craig Cathcart]].  In the 10th minute, Mahrez won a [[Corner kick|corner]] for City which was cleared by Watford who went on the counter-attack.  A cross from Deulofeu found [[Roberto Pereyra]] whose shot was saved by the Manchester City goalkeeper [[Ederson (footballer, born 1993)|Ederson]].  Bernardo Silva then saw his strike saved by Gomes two minutes later and on 16 minutes, City won another corner off Holebas.  Gomes failed to gather the [[Set piece (football)|set piece]] but Watford cleared the ball.<ref name=guardlive3/>  

In the 21st minute, Pereyra passed to [[Abdoulaye Doucouré]] in the Manchester City box, whose shot struck [[Vincent Kompany]]'s arm.  After consultation with the VAR, Friend declined to award a penalty and showed Doucouré the first [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] of the game for his subsequent protests.  Two minutes later, Sterling was unable to capitalise on a Gomes handling error before Jesus' shot was deflected for a corner by Mariappa.  Capoue cleared the ball out to Deulofeu on the [[Glossary of association football terms#B|break]], but Ederson was quick to react and clear the danger. In the 26th minute the deadlock was broken, as David Silva scored his first goal in 28 games, shooting across Gomes from a Sterling header.  On 33 minutes, Mahrez passed to Jesus who was prevented from shooting by a Mariappa [[Tackle (football move)#Association football|tackle]].  Five minutes later Manchester City doubled their lead.  Bernardo Silva played a ball into a space on the left side of the six-yard box for Jesus who side-footed past [[Kiko Femenía]] and Gomes in the Watford goal.  A minute later, Gomes pushed a Mahrez shot away and in the 44th minute, Watford's Hughes struck from distance and the ball was deflected for a corner.  The set piece came to nothing and the half ended with Manchester City holding a 2–0 lead.<ref name=guardlive2/>

===Second half===
No changes were made to either side during half time, and Watford kicked the second half off.  They had the first chance, after 47 minutes, when Deeney found Pereyra who chose to try to find Hughes instead of shooting.  Sterling then found Jesus whose shot from a tight angle was saved by Gomes.  [[Oleksandr Zinchenko (footballer)|Oleksandr Zinchenko]] then crossed for Jesus who headed the ball into the Watford net but the goal was disallowed for offside.  Watford then had a brief spell of pressure but failed to capitalise.  In the 55th minute, Manchester City made their first substitution of the afternoon with De Bruyne coming on to replace Mahrez.  [[İlkay Gündoğan]]'s corner on 57 minutes found Laporte whose header was wide.  Watford responded with Deulofeu picking up a long ball but his shot was mishit wide of the far post.  David Silva was then shown a yellow card in the 60th minute for a [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)|foul]] on Hughes.<ref name=guardlive2/>  On 61 minutes City further extended their lead to 3–0 with a goal from De Bruyne.  Jesus beat Pereyra in the air and passed to De Bruyne, who took the ball past Gomes and scored from close range.<ref name=guardlive/>

In the 65th minute, Watford made a double substitution with Deulofeu and Pereyra coming off, to be replaced by Gray and Success.  Three minutes later, Jesus made it 4–0 after taking the ball on the counter-attack and shooting past Gomes.  De Bruyne then shot high and wide in the 70th minute before Sané was brought on for Gündogan and Watford's Hughes was replaced by [[Tom Cleverley]].  After a period of City possession, [[John Stones]] then came on for David Silva, and in the 80th minute, Kiko was booked for a foul on Sané.  In the 81st minute, Sterling scored from a Bernardo Silva cross to make it 5–0, before scoring again in the 87th minute after his initial shot was pushed onto the post by Gomes.  Two minutes into injury time, Stones' strike from around {{convert|10|yd}} was saved by Gomes and Friend blew the final whistle, ending the match with Manchester City winning 6–0.<ref name=guardlive/>

